      "message": "you don\u0027t :( http://docs.python.org/2/library/socket.html#socket.error\n\nI provided a bit of a bad example in the last patchset... I was just trying to illustrate that you can pass a string to sys.exit() to achieve a clean non-zero exit.\n\nLooking at the docs for socket.error, it looks like you can do:\n\n  \u003e\u003e\u003e import socket\n  \u003e\u003e\u003e sys.exit(socket.error(98, \u0027Address already in use\u0027))\n  [Errno 98] Address already in use\n\nwhich cleanly renders the last line of the backtrace to stdout. So, in this case, this line could be written as:\n\n  sys.exit(e)",
